    "case_id": "518",
    "case_summary": "A healthy 27-year-old Hispanic man presented with a 3-month history of daily fevers with chills, night sweats, generalized weakness, a 25-pound weight loss, poor appetite, and a sore throat. No history of recent infections, trauma, surgery, or travel. Review of systems was negative for shortness of breath, cough, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and changes in bowel or urinary habits.\n\nTemperature: 37.8 °C, heart rate: 118 beats/minute with regular rhythm, blood pressure: 103/67 mm Hg, respiratory rate: 18 cycles/min. Cardiovascular and pulmonary examination were unremarkable. Right knee effusion present with no other inflamed joints. Nonpruritic, palpable, brown macular rash on the chest and back noted.\n\nLaboratory Tests:\n- Leukocyte count: 28.7 × 10⁹/L (normal: 3.5–10.5)\n- Hemoglobin: 87 g/L (normal: 135–175)\n- Platelet count: 750 × 10⁹/L (normal: 150–450)\n- Serum electrolyte and renal function: Normal\n- Aspartate aminotransferase: 2.02 µkat/L (normal: 0.17–0.67)\n- Alanine aminotransferase: 1.79 µkat/L (normal: ≤0.91)\n- Alkaline phosphatase: 6.97 µkat/L (normal: 0.67–2.5)\n- Lactate dehydrogenase: 7.52 µkat/L (normal: 1.98–3.92)\n- Ferritin: 29,161 µg/L (normal: 22–275)\n\nSynovial Fluid Analysis:\n- Leukocyte count: 10.1 × 10⁹/L with 97% neutrophils\n- Cultures: Negative\n\nImaging:\n- CT scan: Diffuse hepatomegaly\n\nBiopsy Results:\n- Skin biopsy: Eosinophilic spongiosis with intraepidermal dyskeratotic keratinocytes\n- Pericardial tissue: Marked acute inflammation with prominent neutrophilic infiltrates and numerous histiocytes",
